Subject: wasix: do less on the syscall entry path when nothing is pending

Every WASIX syscall begins with do_pending_operations, which asks the
thread and the process whether anything has come up. What is left of
that question, now that the signal queue and the exit status answer from
atomics, is three reads of the instance handles and, for a guest that
has installed a signal handler, a lock on the process inner state taken
on every call. A profile of a guest under load put
process_signals_internal, which is where that lock is taken, at 4% of
the runtime's user-mode cycles, more than the clock syscall many of
those calls go on to make.

The lock is taken to look at signal_intervals, the timed signals a
guest asks for with proc_raise_interval. The map is empty unless the
guest asked for one, and almost none do, but the scan for a due signal
locks the same state that thread bookkeeping and checkpoints use.
Publish how many entries the map holds in an atomic beside the lock,
written by signal_interval while it holds the lock, and read it before
the scan. The count a reader sees is one the map had; a reader that
races an install sees the count from one side of the change or the
other, exactly as a read an instant earlier would have, and the next
syscall, which every guest makes in short order, picks the new signal
up. This is the shape SharedMemoryMappings already uses for the futex
path.

The instance handles are read three times over that path: once by
process_signals_and_exit to see whether a handler is installed, once
by process_signals to see the same thing, and once by
process_signals_internal to take the handler. Read them once, into the
two facts the path needs, and pass those down. On the backend where
the handles live behind a thread-local table that is a lookup, a
borrow and a reference count each time; process_signals, which nothing
else called, folds into the shared step. With no signal queued and no
timed signal to be due, the path now stops before the handler is
reached at all, and answers exactly as it did before: the handler was
installed, and no signal was delivered to it.

The slow paths are untouched. Once a signal is queued or a timed
signal exists, the same lock, the same scan and the same handler call
run as they did.

Unit tests cover the count following an install and a removal, one
timed signal not hiding another, every handle of a process seeing what
another set, and a removal that removes nothing announcing nothing.

